## android 11 emulator overlay menu

Mod menu that runs **inside the emulator guest**, drawn on top of the game's own
frame, opened by tapping the red strip pinned to the bottom edge of the screen.

Two ways in. Route A needs no root, so there is nothing for a root check to find.

```powershell
# A - repack the APK, .so loads itself as the app's own uid. no root, no ptrace.
.\build\build_android.ps1 -Abi x86_64
.\build\repack_apk.ps1 -Apk C:\games\base.apk -Package com.example.game -Install

# B - ptrace injection into a rooted guest (lab only, trips root detection)
.\build\build_android.ps1
.\build\build_win.ps1
.\out\win\nhdeploy.exe --package com.example.game --watch --log
```

| path | what |
|---|---|
| `repack/` | Java glue packed into the APK: ContentProvider loader + `Window.Callback` touch mirror |
| `device/menu/` | `libnhmenu.so` - Dobby hook on `eglSwapBuffers`, ImGui overlay, JNI or evdev touch |
| `device/injector/` | `nhinject` - on-device ptrace `.so` injector (route B) |
| `win/nhdeploy/` | Windows CLI for route B: adb, rooting, push, inject, `--watch`, `--unload` |

The overlay hooks `eglSwapBuffers` and draws into the game's back buffer before
the swap, so it needs no `SYSTEM_ALERT_WINDOW` and no overlay permission. Touch
comes from a wrapped `Window.Callback` when there is no root, or straight from
`/dev/input` when there is.

Full setup, root-detection breakdown and gotchas: [docs/INJECT.md](docs/INJECT.md)
